---
layout: layouts/base.njk
wrap: wrap-narrow
pageKey: about
navKey: about
crumbKey: crumb.about
title: "About Citere"
description: "Citere is a Ukrainian research company testing public AI chatbots for Russian disinformation about Ukraine, and working to get what it finds corrected."
pagination:
  data: build.languages
  alias: lang
  size: 1
permalink: "{{ '/about/' | loc(lang) }}"
---
{#- Bilingual About. The site chrome (nav, footer, breadcrumbs) stays in the
    page's own language; only the About body switches between English and
    Ukrainian, via a CSS-only :target toggle — no JavaScript, both texts ship in
    the DOM so crawlers see both. The two #en/#uk targets precede the body so the
    ~ sibling rules in site.css can flip it. Exactly one <h1> (two inner spans),
    so the pre-publish one-h1 check still passes. -#}
{%- set en = copy.en.about -%}
{%- set uk = copy.uk.about or copy.en.about -%}
<i id="en" class="lang-target" aria-hidden="true"></i><i id="uk" class="lang-target" aria-hidden="true"></i>
<nav class="langswitch" aria-label="Language / Мова">
  <a href="#en" class="ls-en" lang="en">English</a>
  <a href="#uk" class="ls-uk" lang="uk">Українська</a>
</nav>
<div class="phead">
  <h1><span class="lg-en">{{ en.data.h1 }}</span><span class="lg-uk" lang="uk">{{ uk.data.h1 }}</span></h1>
  <p class="plead"><span class="lg-en">{{ en.data.lead | mdInline | safe }}</span><span class="lg-uk" lang="uk">{{ uk.data.lead | mdInline | safe }}</span></p>
</div>
<div class="prose prose-en">
{{ en.html | fill(figures | default({})) | prefixLinks | defangCopy | safe }}
</div>
<div class="prose prose-uk" lang="uk">
{{ uk.html | fill(figures | default({})) | prefixLinks | defangCopy | safe }}
</div>
<div style="height:40px"></div>
